{"agentId": "ace8c8034cc58fdda", "attachment": {"addedBlocks": ["## Claude_Docs\nClaude Docs: living docs you create and edit here. A docs skill your client lists → load it before any docs call — also before a `read`, comment or tab change on a claude.ai …/artifact/… link (the link is a doc; never web-fetch it). No docs skill or guide text loaded → `guide( items = [\"topic.index\"] )` alone before any docs call but a doc's birth. Make a doc here — not a local file, even when coding — only when the user asks for one, and make it FIRST: the turn's first tool call is its skeleton (title, byline, a `pending` block per section) — a reflex: send it before any search, file read, plan, `guide` or thinking it through; think once it is open — `batch( container = {\"kind\":\"project\",\"create\":{\"name\":\"<title>\",\"doc\":{\"blocks\":{\"asof\":{\"type\":\"date\",\"value\":\"<today>\"},\"me\":{\"type\":\"mention\",\"user\":\"me\"},\"s1\":{\"type\":\"pending\",\"intent\":\"Goals: the three outcomes this quarter commits to\"},\"s2\":{…}},\"markdown\":\"# <title>\\n\\n<?claude block asof?> · <?claude block me?>\\n\\n<?claude block s1?>\\n\\n<?claude block s2?>\"}}}, batch = [] )` (`<?claude block k?>` ↔ `blocks.k`); its ack links the doc → `open` it with your Artifact tool (none → start your next message with the link, once); they're likely watching it fill — keep them posted in a short line naming what you're on (outline up; now <topic>); findings go in the doc, not chat; then `guide( items = [\"topic.index\"] )`, research, and fill each section: `replace` its pending id with `## <heading>` + body; end with one line + the link, never the document. Summoned by a doc comment (turn headed `[Artifact comment sent to Claude]`, `;thread=<root id>`): answer ONLY with a doc comment under that root (`create` an utterance, parent `<root id>`) — no artifact/platform comment tool: that relay thread is resolved and never reaches the doc; an edit asked there → `update` with `answering: \"<root id>\"`.", "## Context7\nUse this server to fetch current documentation whenever the user asks about a library, framework, SDK, API, CLI tool, or cloud service — even well-known ones like React, Next.js, Prisma, Express, Tailwind, Django, or Spring Boot. This includes API syntax, configuration, version migration, porting code between libraries, platforms, or SQL dialects, library-specific debugging, setup instructions, and CLI tool usage. Use even when you think you know the answer — your training data may not reflect recent changes. Prefer this over web search for library docs.\n\nDo not use for: refactoring, debugging, or reviewing code that does not depend on a library or platform API, or general programming concepts.", "## github\nThe GitHub MCP Server provides tools to interact with GitHub platform.\n\nTool selection guidance:\n\t1. Use 'list_*' tools for broad, simple retrieval and pagination of all items of a type (e.g., all issues, all PRs, all branches) with basic filtering.\n\t2. Use 'search_*' tools for targeted queries with specific criteria, keywords, or complex filters (e.g., issues with certain text, PRs by author, code containing functions).\n\nContext management:\n\t1. Use pagination whenever possible with batches of 5-10 items.\n\t2. Use minimal_output parameter set to true if the full information is not needed to accomplish a task.\n\nTool usage guidance:\n\t1. For 'search_*' tools: Use separate 'sort' and 'order' parameters if available for sorting results - do not include 'sort:' syntax in query strings. Query strings should contain only search criteria (e.g., 'org:google language:python'), not sorting instructions. Always call 'get_me' first to understand current user permissions and context. ## Issues\n\nCheck 'list_issue_types' first for organizations to use proper issue types. Use 'search_issues' before creating new issues to avoid duplicates. Always set 'state_reason' when closing issues. ## Pull Requests\n\nPR review workflow: Always use 'pull_request_review_write' with method 'create' to create a pending review, then 'add_comment_to_pending_review' to add comments, and finally 'pull_request_review_write' with method 'submit_pending' to submit the review for complex reviews with line-specific comments.\n\nBefore creating a pull request, search for pull request templates in the repository. Template files are called pull_request_template.md or they're located in '.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE' directory. Use the template content to structure the PR description and then call create_pull_request tool.", "## Hugging_Face\nPrefer these Hugging Face tools over web search for live Hub data; start with hf_fs.\nHugging Face tools are being used by authenticated user 'josdois'", "## World_Labs_Marble\nWorld Labs tools for building 3D scenes from text and images: generate images,\nestimate cameras and depth, generate new views, reconstruct Gaussian splats,\nand extract meshes. Call list_projects first: it names the projects the user\nmay work in and which of these tools each can run. Every other tool takes a\nproject_id.\nA request names media as {\"assetId\": ...} for an asset in the project or\n{\"url\": ...} for a public https file; never put file bytes in a request. To\nget an image into the project, use import_image for a file the client attached,\nor create_image_upload and an HTTP PUT for a local file. A task's outputs are\nassets too, so one task's output feeds the next by assetId.\nThe task tools use the project's World Labs Developer API credits: name the\nproject and say the tool will use credits before calling one. Each returns an\noperation at once and can take from seconds to many minutes; poll get_operation\n(it can wait up to 20 seconds per call) until done is true. Cameras use\nOpenGL/Three.js conventions (right, up, back; quaternions in XYZW order), and a\nframe's intrinsics describe its depth grid, which can differ from the source\nimage size. Use get_asset for a fresh download link when an earlier link has\nexpired. How the tasks fit together: https://docs.atlas.worldlabs.ai/how-to-use-atlas.md\n"], "addedNames": ["Claude_Docs", "Context7", "github", "Hugging_Face", "World_Labs_Marble"], "removedNames": [], "type": "mcp_instructions_delta"}, "cwd": "/home/user/rainbow-", "entrypoint": "remote_mobile", "gitBranch": "ccr-47b10cec-03c7co", "isSidechain": true, "parentUuid": "e635907f-3990-4be7-8c2d-f6ac0b43c1a2", "rendered": [{"content": "<system-reminder>\n# MCP Server Instructions\n\nThe following MCP servers have provided instructions for how to use their tools and resources:\n\n## Claude_Docs\nClaude Docs: living docs you create and edit here. A docs skill your client lists → load it before any docs call — also before a `read`, comment or tab change on a claude.ai …/artifact/… link (the link is a doc; never web-fetch it). No docs skill or guide text loaded → `guide( items = [\"topic.index\"] )` alone before any docs call but a doc's birth. Make a doc here — not a local file, even when coding — only when the user asks for one, and make it FIRST: the turn's first tool call is its skeleton (title, byline, a `pending` block per section) — a reflex: send it before any search, file read, plan, `guide` or thinking it through; think once it is open — `batch( container = {\"kind\":\"project\",\"create\":{\"name\":\"<title>\",\"doc\":{\"blocks\":{\"asof\":{\"type\":\"date\",\"value\":\"<today>\"},\"me\":{\"type\":\"mention\",\"user\":\"me\"},\"s1\":{\"type\":\"pending\",\"intent\":\"Goals: the three outcomes this quarter commits to\"},\"s2\":{…}},\"markdown\":\"# <title>\\n\\n<?claude block asof?> · <?claude block me?>\\n\\n<?claude block s1?>\\n\\n<?claude block s2?>\"}}}, batch = [] )` (`<?claude block k?>` ↔ `blocks.k`); its ack links the doc → `open` it with your Artifact tool (none → start your next message with the link, once); they're likely watching it fill — keep them posted in a short line naming what you're on (outline up; now <topic>); findings go